d "c0961860-b611-5cd7-8889-52b60fa3a9fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-51433" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/c9373fff819b3c54ac7aa776642019ac7d4023656aa586d70b3899f821fcfd2c.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. What are the points of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "The points of inflection are the points where \n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph. \n\n- The point $x=-2$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=-2.$ Second, the function is not defined at this point, so the tangent line does not exist either.\n\n- The point $x=1$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave up to concave down at $x=1,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=3$ is not a point of inflection. Although the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=3,$  the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\nTherefore, the point of inflection is $x=1$ only." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-51433/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0099e032-f2ee-573a-a359-cf60e28bc89e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-51433"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-114280/selection/565f087efc172cc689d6ac7a38ae1818e245a6f8995ecba68bcdf0ac5950b2ea" :answer/id #uuid "4ff2a7df-759d-5e41-9c72-425405692344" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114280/selection/8dd0139fe2bdff82a7de02af21e81b3fb0031e695d60709e4cec3b9cfa395bee" :answer/id #uuid "a6c4dc61-c630-59bb-a18c-49f1da03d6f8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 