per/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/c3b95c16aff03448b59619ba6d966a4ff645f5952468b8c2701f526185c43a64.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. What are the points of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "The points of inflection are the points where \n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph. \n\n- The point $x=0$ is not a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave down both to the left and to the right of $x=0.$ Second, the function is not defined at this point, so the tangent line does not exist either.\n\n- The point $x=1$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=1,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=4$ is not a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=4.$ Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\nTherefore, the point of inflection is $x=1$ only." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-51429/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0099e032-f2ee-573a-a359-cf60e28bc89e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-51429"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-114399/selection/be41bc64385bf521340524b4e278e6df8cc0ad1e10dce19632624b00d7fa202b" :answer/id #uuid "953af0b3-e502-5714-a019-e20e99c31738" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=1$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114399/selection/240e0d528c486a69fa85d43e08aa7e442ec4d45679b0ce5d8c3e3adb10b0b26b" :answer/id #uuid "785c8a1d-26f5-59bc-9e01-c2cb2bcf8afe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=1$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114399/selection/8dd0139fe2bdff82a7de02af21e