pe :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344059/field-1/608b697f811192589eec3729e28e5d056be7cd5127af1a846d717a33508ae203" :answer/id #uuid "41299c08-50bd-5d16-b696-a7f8f54383b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344059/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "55a6ba74-1094-5ea7-91fe-193e75670bc4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344059/field-1/20fe9348ed46a2e53aa93407d23f518ddb4c3d338901026ade2e49c11e8c32ae" :answer-field/choices ["q-344059/field-1/20fe9348ed46a2e53aa93407d23f518ddb4c3d338901026ade2e49c11e8c32ae" "q-344059/field-1/608b697f811192589eec3729e28e5d056be7cd5127af1a846d717a33508ae203"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344059/field-2/138caba1686f50a765c301fd0e85704fa29e1cfce953a0c8868b005ad608ecff" :answer/id #uuid "5378ee04-60fc-5d28-bf07-50e946d8b254" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344059/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "fb710636-c076-538d-acb6-757c880357f6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344059/field-2/138caba1686f50a765c301fd0e85704fa29e1cfce953a0c8868b005ad608ecff" :answer-field/choices ["q-344059/field-2/138caba1686f50a765c301fd0e85704fa29e1cfce953a0c8868b005ad608ecff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344059" :question/id #uuid "f4d32a7d-f41e-5649-9959-0b6167c0aa1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-344059" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the $x$-coordinates of the points of inflection for $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+x$ on the interval $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,\\text{and}\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0$ on the interval $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}.$ So, we pick points around them, say $x=\\frac{\\pi }{16},$ $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{16}) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-16\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=-16\\cdot (-1)>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )=-16\\cdot (1)<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{8},$ we conclude that, in ascending order, \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\n$$\n\n are indeed points of inflection." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-344059/field-1" "q-344059/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "841151db-1c26-5059-9796-75b70e41f659"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344059"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344064/field-1/5ab3573096b30d3c5d3c378e667364cbb062f921e9b1002441e594f0774a669d" :answer/id #uuid "8ab06a09-0a9e-5b78-99e9-7199790a1ae7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344064/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "33cc9e92-05cd-5f1b-8051-2026a6ea8e03" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344064/field-1/5ab3573096b30d3c5d3c378e667364cbb062f921e9b1002441e594f0774a669d" :answer-field/choices ["q-344064/field-1/5ab3573096b30d3c5d3c378e667364cbb062f921e9b1002441e594f0774a669d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344064" :question/id #uuid "0bcd2b1f-395b-56e8-a871-9409daffb768" :question/math-academy-id "q-344064" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=27{x}^{2}+5x-4+\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, notice that $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=0,$ so this can't be a point of inflection.\n\nNow, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =54x+5-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =54+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}54+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =-\\frac{1}{27} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-\\frac{1}{3}.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=-1$ and $x=-\\frac{1}{6}$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1) & =54+\\frac{2}{(-1)^{3}}=52>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(-\\frac{1}{6}) & =54+\\frac{2}{{(-\\frac{1}{6})}^{3}}=-378<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we conclude that $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ is indeed a point of inflection." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-344064/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "841151db-1c26-5059-9796-75b70e41f659"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344064"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-114409/selection/0bf5abed43f6f20dd466b36279de8736a04c540ff5306cb048ae22803a7b3f9c" :answer/id #uuid "207693c9-f7ed-5426-965e-0eaa4abfef8e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114409/selection/483e7314b7e91870e67cf06a88a29e4311cd8f6702748ab8cca9bb83e249a7a8" :answer/id #uuid "710bf677-8835-519b-9ed4-b3d18cffa733" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114409/selection/9f75d63bbce36d71b1687120236f1905d3381f1a10419d07fe4d39d3b5a3b550" :answer/id #uuid "4d330736-5341-57cc-8983-c076710d7377" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114409/selection/02c2ac9730c208f517b45425f4e43eb110953149e4da3e6c03d6ca5b06cd40f6" :answer/id #uuid "ee74414b-3424-54e3-90b9-e4e79fd4e817" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114409/selection/9705668966fb8d1af3eb916ffbf68287f9daac20760c8675176a76fe3a09d29a" :answer/id #uuid "9fe4a658-2d3e-5b4e-afc9-8bcaa3273da8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114409/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "aa8d8d52-727d-5d36-bb07-9153d5ee2189" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-114409/selection/9705668966fb8d1af3eb916ffbf68287f9daac20760c8675176a76fe3a09d29a" :answer-field/choices ["q-114409/selection/0bf5abed43f6f20dd466b36279de8736a04c540ff5306cb048ae22803a7b3f9c" "q-114409/selection/483e7314b7e91870e67cf06a88a29e4311cd8f6702748ab8cca9bb83e249a7a8" "q-114409/selection/9f75d63bbce36d71b1687120236f1905d3381f1a10419d07fe4d39d3b5a3b550" "q-114409/selection/02c2ac9730c208f517b45425f4e43eb110953149e4da3e6c03d6ca5b06cd40f6" "q-114409/selection/9705668966fb8d1af3eb916ffbf68287f9daac20760c8675176a76fe3a09d29a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-114409" :question/id #uuid "ba4f29a4-295c-5fc5-b7f4-771d4a403940" :question/math-academy-id "q-114409" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points of inflection for $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{2\\cdot ({x}^{2}+1)-2x\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-{x}^{2})}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(1-{x}^{2})}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 1-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (1-x)(1+x) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=-1$  and $x=1.$ So, we pick points around them, say $x=-2,$ $x=0$ and $x=2,$ and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2) & =\\frac{2(1-(-2)^{2})}{(1+(-2)^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{25}<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =\\frac{2(1-(0)^{2})}{(1+(0)^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =2>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =\\frac{2(1-(2)^{2})}{(1+(2)^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{25}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-1$ and $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=-1$ and $x=1$ are indeed points of inflection." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-114409/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "841151db-1c26-5059-9796-75b70e41f659"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-114409"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-314762/field-1/257a2afc22734cbfc05cf0e198a5fab4417366a332dfe0ea2a385a765174fbcc" :answer/id #uuid "0e60e2a7-882f-5460-b47b-f6fba84c3cb8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-314762/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "a43b4729-c47f-5a47-a86d-c26a8fddf152" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-314762/field-1/257a2afc22734cbfc05cf0e198a5fab4417366a332dfe0ea2a385a765174fbcc" :answer-field/choices ["q-314762/field-1/257a2afc22734cbfc05cf0e198a5fab4417366a332dfe0ea2a385a765174fbcc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-314762" :question/id #uuid "1d707847-ca08-55f0-a593-95ee79b1454f" :question/math-academy-id "q-314762" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)={e}^{-1/{x}^{2}}$ defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}{e}^{-1/{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}){e}^{-1/{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}){e}^{-1/{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{6}}){e}^{-1/{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{6}}\\,(2-3{x}^{2})\\,{e}^{-1/{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{{x}^{6}}(2-3{x}^{2}){e}^{-1/{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we ignored the negative square root since $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}.$ So, we pick two points around it, say $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=1$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}\\,(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{2}{{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{6}}\\,(2-3\\,{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}){e}^{-1/(1/2)^{2}} \\\\  & =128\\,(2-\\frac{3}{4}){e}^{-4} \\\\  & =160{e}^{-4}>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =\\frac{2}{1}\\,(2-3)\\,{e}^{-1} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-1}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}},$ we conclude that $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}$ is indeed a point of inflection." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-314762/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "841151db-1c26-5059-9796-75b70e41f659"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-314762"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344063/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "244a8ea8-991a-5546-a01e-f67f4b8c06c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344063/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b3f11e8f-09c4-58ef-846e-f3450b62633c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344063/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-344063/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344063" :question/id #uuid "6de3588d-45bf-5afc-a746-6941c52fd3d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-344063" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)={x}^{2}+3x-2+\\frac{1}{x-2}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, notice that $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=2,$ so this can't be a point of inflection.\n\nNow, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x+3-\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2+\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{3}} & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{3} & =-1 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=1.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{3}{2}$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =2+\\frac{2}{(0-2)^{3}}=\\frac{7}{4}>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{3}{2}) & =2+\\frac{2}{{(\\frac{3}{2}-2)}^{3}}=-14<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=1$ is indeed a point of inflection." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-344063/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "841151db-1c26-5059-9796-75b70e41f659"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344063"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344246/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "c0855285-378e-5c34-b35e-85aabd09a19d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344246/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "a7c0c38c-7dd0-5344-bcb5-cf9e5fb0e6d1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344246/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer-field/choices ["q-344246/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344246" :question/id #uuid "45f9354a-20d2-5917-8cc8-23e78cc4519e" :question/math-academy-id "q-344246" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection for $g(x)={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-6).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-6)+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}-6x+6) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}) \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3})+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}+3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x+3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-3$ and $x=0.$ So, we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=-4,\\,$ $\\,x=-1,\\,$ and $\\,x=1,$ \n\nand compute the sign of ${g}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(-4) & ={e}^{-4}((-4)^{3}+3(-4)^{2})=-16{e}^{-4}<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(-1) & ={e}^{-1}((-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2})=2{e}^{-1}>0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(1) & ={e}^{1}((1)^{3}+3(1)^{2})=4e>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=-3.$ So, since the concavity changes at $x=-3,$ we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed a point of inflection.\n\n- The second derivative does not change the sign at $x=0.$ So, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=0,$ we conclude that $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection.\n\nTherefore, only  $x=-3$ is an inflection point." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-344246/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a363f215-ed40-504a-bcae-620b7d585678"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344246"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-114407"] :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection for $g(x)={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+26x-36).$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+26x-36)+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}-16x+26) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+10x-10) \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+10x-10)+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}-10x+10) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}-2{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=0$ and $x=2.$ So, we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=-1,\\,$ $\\,x=1,\\,$ and $\\,x=3,$ \n\nand compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(-1) & ={e}^{-1}((-1)^{3}-2(-1)^{2})=-3{e}^{-1}<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(1) & ={e}^{1}((1)^{3}-2(1)^{2})=-e<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(3) & ={e}^{3}((3)^{3}-2(3)^{2})=9{e}^{3}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative does not change the sign at $x=0.$ So, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=0,$ we conclude that $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection.\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=2.$ So, since the concavity changes at $x=2,$ we conclude that $x=2$ is indeed a point of inflection.\n\nTherefore, only $x=2$ is an inflection point." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-344242/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "7d43eef2-ad3c-52f6-acbe-9b69fc17c2ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344242/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "7dd22af7-ab24-5343-8a8e-335f2097c620" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344242/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-344242/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344242" :question/id #uuid "6cae6596-2614-5d20-9d4e-fa89cfe32b05" :question/math-academy-id "q-344242" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection for $g(x)={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+30x-42).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+30x-42)+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}-18x+30) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+12x-12) \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+12x-12)+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}-12x+12) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=0$ and $x=3.$ So, we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=-1,\\,$ $\\,x=1,\\,$ and $\\,x=4,$ \n\nand compute the sign of ${g}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(-1) & ={e}^{-1}((-1)^{3}-3(-1)^{2})=-4{e}^{-1}<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(1) & ={e}^{1}((1)^{3}-3(1)^{2})=-2e<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(4) & ={e}^{4}((4)^{3}-3(4)^{2})=16{e}^{4}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative does not change the sign at $x=0.$ So, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=0,$ we conclude that $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection.\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=3.$ So, since the concavity changes at $x=3,$ we conclude that $x=3$ is indeed a point of inflection.\n\nTherefore, only  $x=3$ is an inflection point." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-344242/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a363f215-ed40-504a-bcae-620b7d585678"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344242"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344226/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "956d3eda-66ca-5711-a004-a0a0a317c39e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344226/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "1147df65-da1d-5798-87e1-458a70c9229c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344226/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "837b0288-ce4d-522c-8a5a-7f7ecbe97848" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344226/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer-field/choices ["q-344226/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" "q-344226/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344226/field-2/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "3ffbdbb3-ccc3-5411-bd12-adfb9215bcd4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344226/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "b55743b5-67fe-5439-85c0-da800c3ece5d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344226/field-2/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer-field/choices ["q-344226/field-2/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344226" :question/id #uuid "59971680-0450-5fd1-ac8d-9e7399bdc2a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-344226" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection for $g(x)={x}^{4}+10{x}^{3}+24{x}^{2}-6x$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,\\text{and}\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2}+48x-6 \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & =12{x}^{2}+60x+48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{2}+60x+48 & =0 \\\\ 12(x+4)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-4$ and $x=-1.$ So we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=-5,\\,$ $x=-2,\\,$ and $x=0,$\n\n and compute the sign of ${g}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(-5) & =12(-5)^{2}+60(-5)+48=48>0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(-2) & =12(-2)^{2}+60(-2)+48=-24<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(0) & =12(0)^{2}+60(0)+48=48>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=-4$ and $x=-1.$Therefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-4$ and $x=-1,$ we conclude that $x=-4$ and $x=-1$ are indeed  points of inflection.\n\nTherefore,  $x=-4$ and $x=-1$ are points of inflection." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-344226/field-1" "q-344226/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a363f215-ed40-504a-bcae-620b7d585678"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344226"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-79208/selection/84ea79387d51fde1e84146322c8639e6e5eae9a28be16c7c74f23a3fe78716de" :answer/id #uuid "aa0bef79-8db6-5bbe-b576-e372add96cc6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There are no points of inflection" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79208/selection/6bac05a62229f29eba4498544257eb386a58da0ea736d3d95ca346c883c43950" :answer/id #uuid "a7556218-3427-500f-a7e8-2f4986b22d27" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79208/selection/3156fa3aa006e591d496860456be38662b872375433bbcf5b2f4cb01e08eaa8b" :answer/id #uuid "fc0fdb3e-9929-5a8a-8a22-ffa688292a73" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79208/selection/005c66648f6a5099b49c4a3aef5ae5d62b64c56b2537481ae27f1c644383047d" :answer/id #uuid "ad6b7a61-ecd1-57c1-85f4-726a7d723114" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79208/selection/8abec89cc12117b8c2d7e44b0bdb69f3275de04bec1f8e63c103563329debc8b" :answer/id #uuid "09bea8f4-2722-5e62-9ff1-edf383b8a9f3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-3,$ $x=0$ and $x=3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79208/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d42a49c5-8573-50af-a9e2-c60c4e2bcbba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-79208/selection/3156fa3aa006e591d496860456be38662b872375433bbcf5b2f4cb01e08eaa8b" :answer-field/choices ["q-79208/selection/84ea79387d51fde1e84146322c8639e6e5eae9a28be16c7c74f23a3fe78716de" "q-79208/selection/6bac05a62229f29eba4498544257eb386a58da0ea736d3d95ca346c883c43950" "q-79208/selection/3156fa3aa006e591d496860456be38662b872375433bbcf5b2f4cb01e08eaa8b" "q-79208/selection/005c66648f6a5099b49c4a3aef5ae5d62b64c56b2537481ae27f1c644383047d" "q-79208/selection/8abec89cc12117b8c2d7e44b0bdb69f3275de04bec1f8e63c103563329debc8b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-79208" :question/id #uuid "22d8eb63-a7a6-5f30-b326-a85e616affbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-79208" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection for $g(x)={x}^{5}+5{x}^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =5{x}^{4}+20{x}^{3} \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & =20{x}^{3}+60{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{x}^{3}+60{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 20{x}^{2}(x+3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-3$ and $x=0.$ So we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=-4,\\,$ $x=-1,\\,$ and $x=1,$\n\n and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(-4) & =20(-4)^{3}+60(-4)^{2}=-320<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(-1) & =20(-1)^{3}+60(-1)^{2}=40>0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(1) & =20(1)^{3}+60(1)^{2}=80>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=-3.$ Therefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-3,$ we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed a point of inflection.\n\n- The second derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=0,$ we conclude that $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection.\n\nTherefore, only $x=-3$ is a point of inflection." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-79208/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a363f215-ed40-504a-bcae-620b7d585678"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-79208"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3739"] :question/problem "Consider the function $y = f(x)$ below, defined on $x\\in(-2,8).$ At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/54d2fe17d8d36a23c7c714242ffbfa12a56fd8e870b7672f1ce9b7e02f6508b4.png)" :question/worked-solution "The points of inflection are the points where\n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph. \n\n- The point $x=0$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=0,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=2$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=2.$ Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\n- The point $x=4$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave up to concave down at $x=4,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=6$ is *not* a point of inflection. Although the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x = 6,$  the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\nTherefore, the points of inflection are $x=0$ and $x=4$ only." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3742"] :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y = f''(x)$ is shown below. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/6b146f4a7bec69f1e984b3e2aa0e3c38ea65c4464da037759b41eac2fd97325f.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative $f''(x)$ must switch sign. So, we are looking for points where the graph of $f''(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y=f''(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x = -4$ and $x=0.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-4$ and $x=0.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3746"] :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection (if any) for $f(x) = x^3 + 9x^2 + 24 x + 22.$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation $f''(x) =0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tf(x) & = x^3 + 9x^2 + 24 x + 22\\\n \t\tf'(x) & = 3x^2 + 18x + 24 \\\n \t\tf''(x) & = 6x + 18\n}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve $f''(x) = 0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t6x+ 18 &= 0\\\n \t\t6x  &= -18\\\n \t\tx &= -3.\n}\n$$\n\nFinally,  we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-3.$ So we pick two points around it, say,  $x=-4$ and $x=-2$, and compute the sign of $f''(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-4) & =6(-4)+18=-6<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(-2) & =6(-2)+18=6>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-3,$ we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed a point of inflection." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3747"] :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of all points of inflection for $f(x) = x^4 - 4x^3 + 6 x^2.$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation $f''(x) =0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}+12x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =12{x}^{2}-24x+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve $f''(x) = 0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{2}-24x+12 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=1.$ So we pick two points around it, say, \n\n$\\qquad$ $x=0 \\quad$ and $\\quad x=2,$ \n\nand compute the sign of $f''(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =12(0)^{2}-24(0)+12=12>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =12(2)^{2}-24(2)+12=12>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=1$ is *not* a point of inflection." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
